    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d71">

                <pb facs="0182" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v19/pg156"/>

                <head>71. Telegram From the Embassy in India to the Department of State<anchor n="0"
                        xml:id="d71-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>New Delhi</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1961-12-13T16:00:00+05:30">December 13, 1961,
                            4 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1730. Following telegram 145 from Karachi repeated for your information:</p>
                <p>For Timmons.</p>
                <p>Would you deliver to Nehru’s office the following letter to Nehru signed by you
                    as Charge:<anchor n="1" xml:id="d71-f2"/></p>
                <p>“Dear Mr. Prime Minister:</p>
                <p>The President has asked the Embassy to tell you of his concern over the spreading
                    rumors that India will resort to force to solve the Goa problem. He is strongly
                    hopeful that India will not follow such a course with its grievous example for
                    the world.</p>
                <p>The President has expressed his approval of Ambassador Galbraith’s letter<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d71-f3"/> with the importance it attaches to separating the
                    use of force from the colonial issues involved in the Portuguese presence.”</p>
                <pb facs="0183" n="157" xml:id="pg157"/>
                <p>Advise Washington and Karachi when message delivered and repeat exact text to
                    Washington.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Timmons</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d71-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d71-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 753D.00/12-1361. Secret; Niact.
                    Repeated to Paris and Lisbon.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d71-f2" n="1" xml:id="d71-f2-content">
                <p>The substance of the President’s message was sent to Galbraith in Karachi, where
                    he was meeting with Rountree and Ayub, for his concurrence. (Telegram 1154 to
                    Karachi, December 12; ibid., 753D.00/12-1261) Galbraith drafted the text of the
                    letter and forwarded it to New Delhi.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d71-f3" n="2" xml:id="d71-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d70-f4">footnote 3, Document 70</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
